1
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:14,813
On a quiet night in the barrio, the frogs were singing and the bamboo creaked in the wind. Little Pina the mouse-deer pulled her blanket up to her big dark eyes.

2
00:00:15,113 --> 00:00:21,636
"Lolo Kanor, my cousin says an aswang lives in the next village. Is it true? What is an aswang?"

3
00:00:21,936 --> 00:00:31,552
Lolo Kanor, the old eagle, chuckled softly. "That story is more than four hundred years old, anak. Come closer, and I will tell you where it began."

4
00:00:32,752 --> 00:00:42,091
In Philippine folklore, aswang is a name for many night creatures at once. It can mean a shapeshifter, a witch, a ghoul, or a vampire.

5
00:00:42,391 --> 00:00:51,708
By day, the story goes, an aswang looks like an ordinary neighbour. By night, it may turn into a black dog, a pig, or a large bird.

6
00:00:52,008 --> 00:00:59,363
One famous kind, the manananggal, leaves the bottom half of its body behind and flies away on bat wings.

7
00:01:00,563 --> 00:01:11,736
The oldest written records come from Spanish colonial writers. In 1582, Miguel de Loarca described a Visayan belief in a creature he wrote as asuang.

8
00:01:12,036 --> 00:01:22,377
In 1589, the friar Juan de Plasencia listed the osuang among Tagalog beliefs: someone said to fly at night and eat human flesh.

9
00:01:22,677 --> 00:01:32,549
Some friars used these stories to paint the old healers, the babaylan, as witches. Many of the women who led rituals were treated as dangerous.

10
00:01:33,749 --> 00:01:40,635
For centuries, the tales passed from grandparent to grandchild, around oil lamps and under nipa roofs.

11
00:01:40,935 --> 00:01:48,609
People said a tiktik bird calls softly when an aswang is near, and that the softer the call, the closer it really is.

12
00:01:48,909 --> 00:01:57,075
To stay safe, families hung garlic, kept salt and vinegar near the door, and carried a buntot pagi, the tail of a stingray.

13
00:01:58,275 --> 00:02:05,224
Over time, one province became tied to the legend more than any other: Capiz, on the island of Panay.

14
00:02:05,524 --> 00:02:14,756
Researchers think trade, rivalry and gossip between regions helped give Capiz that reputation. People there have long found it unfair.

15
00:02:15,056 --> 00:02:22,496
Today Capiz is better known for its seafood and its shining capiz shell windows, but the old joke still follows it.

16
00:02:23,696 --> 00:02:31,755
The legend even reached a real war. In the early nineteen fifties, the government fought the Huk rebels in central Luzon.

17
00:02:32,055 --> 00:02:41,607
American officer Edward Lansdale later wrote that his team spread aswang rumours near a rebel camp, then staged a body to look like a victim.

18
00:02:41,907 --> 00:02:49,688
He claimed the frightened rebels left the area. Historians still debate how much of his story happened exactly as told.

19
00:02:50,888 --> 00:02:58,627
In the nineteen sixties and seventies, folklorist Maximo Ramos collected aswang stories from all over the islands.

20
00:02:58,927 --> 00:03:07,881
He found that the aswang often explained frightening things: a sudden illness, a baby lost before birth, a death no one understood.

21
00:03:08,181 --> 00:03:18,309
Scholars such as Herminia Meñez saw another pattern. The person called aswang was often an outsider, a loner, or someone who was simply different.

22
00:03:18,609 --> 00:03:27,137
No scientist has ever found a real aswang. But the fear it caused was very real, and sometimes innocent people were blamed.

23
00:03:28,337 --> 00:03:36,972
Today the aswang appears in films, comics and games, and it has become one of the best known creatures of Filipino storytelling.

24
00:03:37,272 --> 00:03:45,587
Its stories also kept old knowledge alive: names of plants, rules about the night, and the habit of looking after one another.

25
00:03:45,887 --> 00:03:51,385
"So the aswang is a story that tells us what people were afraid of," said Pina slowly.

26
00:03:52,585 --> 00:04:00,303
"Exactly, anak," said Lolo Kanor. "And a good lesson is never to call someone a monster just because they are different."

27
00:04:00,603 --> 00:04:04,885
Outside, a small bird called from the mango tree. Pina jumped.

28
00:04:05,185 --> 00:04:10,151
Lolo Kanor laughed. "That is only a kuwago, an owl, looking for its supper."

29
00:04:10,451 --> 00:04:16,141
Pina giggled, snuggled close under his wing, and finally closed her big dark eyes.

30
00:04:16,441 --> 00:04:22,452
And in the quiet barrio, the stories kept on whispering, just as they have for four hundred years.
